exg-c — unaffiliated 8-channel EXG host (C)

Build:  make            # libnplearn.a + ./np-exg
        make lib        # matcher only
Run:    ./np-exg
        ./np-exg --cli --port /dev/ttyUSB1 --seconds 8

nplearn/ is a separate no-heap C library (filter + named templates +
score). The SDL host links it; an MCU compiles nplearn.c + nplearn_filt.c
only. See nplearn/README.

Requires libSDL2 and dialout (the host re-execs via sg if needed).
Once:  sudo ./install-usb-permissions.sh
Settings persist in ~/.config/exg-c.ini (window size, colors, view).
Sidebar Settings tab: window size, per-channel colors.

Keys: c connect  d disconnect  r record  space pause  1-8 toggle  q quit
Click View buttons for window, scale, 50/60 Hz notch, high-pass, grid.
Default plot is ±200 µV. After CAL (headset off), a channel still at
that baseline FROZEN — the strip stops scrolling, like the official
host when you take the headset off. Live update resumes when RMS
leaves the baseline. Scale OG is the old stretch-the-rail look.
Learn: click 1. type a name, hold the pose, click Record 2s and stay still.
It captures at the end of the countdown. MATCH scores live vs saved samples.
Same name overwrites. del removes the selected chip.
Learn always runs hp 2 Hz, a 40 Hz low-pass, and the mains notch (50 if
view notch is off). Save records the live window as-is after that filter;
it does not refuse rail or large EMG. Samples persist in ~/.config/exg-c.learn
Firmware on the board is 125 SPS; the status line shows measured rate.

Calibrate: click CAL, take the headset off, click OK. That window is the
open-input baseline (exg-c.cal next to the binary). With CUT on, any
channel still at that baseline is drawn flat (static removed); a real
change vs cal is shown after subtracting the baseline DC. Toggle CUT to
see the raw rail again.

USB frames keep coming when the analog switches are off — those switches
only route the front-end, they do not stop acquire_data(). Open inputs
sit at the ADS1299 rail (~±0.13 V) and are labeled open/rail, not EEG.
Lead-off bits (frame bytes 18–19) are shown when the firmware sets them.
